<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      F16. `pos.tax.manage` — may this person decide what "standard rate" MEANS.

      ══ What the screen is ══

      /app/settings/tax is the tenant's catalogue of sales-tax rates: a code the tax authority
      recognises, a name a person recognises, and a percentage. A menu CATEGORY names one as its
      default and every item in it inherits; one item may override. Nothing else in the product
      sets a rate across more than a single dish.

      Before F16 there was no such screen. The 2026-08-12 walkthrough recorded /app/settings/tax,
      /app/settings/taxes, /app/finance/tax and /app/menu/tax all rendering "This page doesn't
      exist" for the OWNER, and the owner sidebar carrying no tax entry at all. The consequence was
      in that day's own bill: a Rs 1,657.00 dine-in subtotal taxed Rs 25.60 - 1.5% - because two
      Butter Naans carried a rate and no other line on the check did.

      ══ Why this is NOT pos.menu.manage ══

      The two decisions only look alike.

        pos.menu.manage - "this curry is standard-rated". Menu work. Done a dozen times a week by
          whoever adds dishes; MANAGER holds it (changeset 041) and keeps it. It also already
          governs the per-item custom rate, which has been editable in the item dialog since S0-03.

        pos.tax.manage  - "standard rate is 17%". Not menu work. It is a statutory fact about the
          business; when it is wrong it is wrong for every dish at once, and what it mis-states is
          the tax return rather than one bill. That belongs with the person who signs the return.

      Phase 19b established this precedent when it split pos.tables.admin from pos.tables.manage,
      and 28-04 repeated it for pos.terminals.admin: verbs that share a noun and nothing else do
      not share a permission. Reusing pos.menu.manage here would have handed every branch manager
      the tenant's fiscal configuration as a side effect of being allowed to add a dish.

      This widens nothing. Before this changeset no role could set a rate except one item at a
      time, because no screen existed that could.

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN - the same two personas /app/settings already admits (a branch MANAGER
      is refused there today), and the same holder set changesets 089 and 090 chose for the same
      reason. If it turns out a branch manager needs it, the fix is another explicit grant argued
      on its own, not a widening of what this code means.

      MANAGER is deliberately left able to READ the catalogue: TaxClassServiceImpl.list() gates on
      pos.menu.view, because the Menu Items screen already shows them every item's rate and hiding
      the catalogue behind those rates would leave them looking at a "Standard rate" caption with
      no way to find out what it stands for.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Same reason 055, 059, 082, 083, 085, 088, 089 and 090 each give: the blanket "grant every
      pos.* permission to OWNER" statements in changeset 041 are point-in-time and retro-grant
      nothing added after they ran. A permission that no role holds does not fail at startup, does
      not break a test, and produces a clean confident 403 for every user including OWNER - which
      is indistinguishable from a deliberate denial. The verification changeSet below RAISEs rather
      than warns for exactly that reason.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-091-pos-tax-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>pos.tax.manage — define and re-rate the tenant's sales-tax classes; held by OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.tax.manage', 'pos', 'Define, re-rate and retire the sales-tax classes the whole menu is priced against')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.tax.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.tax.manage')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.tax.manage';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.tax.manage';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-091-verify-pos-tax-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if pos.tax.manage exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'pos.tax.manage';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'pos.tax.manage is defined but granted to no role. pos-service refuses every write to /api/v1/pos/tax-classes without it, so the sales-tax screen would render an access refusal for every user on every tenant including OWNER - as an ordinary-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
